# Background

Community Diagnostic Centres (CDCs) are places where people can have healthcare tests, such as scans, away from a hospital. These tests help doctors and nurses find out what may be happening inside a person’s body and whether they need treatment. CDCs aim to make tests easier to access. However, we do not yet know whether they work equally well for everyone. This study will look at the Metrocentre and Workington CDCs. It will also look at how easy they are to access compared to hospital testing services in Newcastle, Gateshead, and North Cumbria.

# Methods

The study has two parts.

First, the research team will study appointment information already collected by the CDCs and hospitals. The information will\
include patients’ age group, sex, ethnic group, level of disadvantage based on where they live, where they live, type of healthcare test, which hospital department sent them for the test, where the test was supposed to take place, and whether the appointment was attended. The researchers will not get patient names, addresses, full postcodes, NHS numbers, or any contact details.

Second, the team will interview approximately 21–33 CDC members of staff and 24–34 adults who have had healthcare tests at the CDCs and local hospitals. Interviews will last about 40–60 minutes. They will take place face to face or on Microsoft Teams and will be audio-recorded. Each person interviewed will receive a £25 voucher as a thank-you for their time.

The researchers will look at both parts of the study together to understand how easy it is for people to get tests, what choices they have, and whether they attend appointments. The results may help the NHS make it easier for people to get healthcare tests.

